# PN (Paradigm Networks) login check

This plugin (`pn-sanitizer`) gates every prompt and tool call through CodeDefense,
which requires this workspace to be logged in to PN. Two other mechanisms try to
surface a missing login, but **neither reliably reaches you (the agent) on the
first message of a session**, so this rule is the backstop:

- `check-session.py` (`sessionStart`) injects context asking you to request the
  base URL, but `sessionStart` is fire-and-forget — the agent loop doesn't wait
  for or enforce it, so it can race with (or miss) the user's first message.
- `check-prompt.py` (`beforeSubmitPrompt`) only supports `continue` /
  `user_message` output fields. `user_message` is a UI-level notice to the
  human when a prompt is blocked — it is never injected into your context, so
  an "allowed" prompt with PN unconfigured gives you no signal to act on.

## What to do

On the **first user message of a session**, before doing anything else, check
whether PN is already configured:

```bash
python3 -c "import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '<plugin-root>/scripts'); import pn_config; print(pn_config.get_valid_access_token())"
```

(substitute the actual plugin root path — the directory containing this
plugin's `scripts/` folder).

- If it prints a tuple (not `None`), PN is already configured. Proceed with
  the user's request normally — no further action needed.
- If it prints `None`, PN is **not** configured. Ask the user for their PN
  base URL (e.g. `https://acme.paradigmnetworks.ai`) before proceeding with
  their request, then run the `pn-login` skill to log in. Do not invent,
  guess, or reuse a base URL from another project or example.

Do not treat an allowed prompt or a "CodeDefense API unreachable" message as
proof that PN is configured — `check-prompt.py` and `check-response.py` fail
open on purpose, so prompts and tool calls can proceed even when there's no
login at all.

You only need to do this once per session — after you've confirmed PN is
configured (or completed a login), don't repeat the check on later messages
unless the user asks to log in again or a hook message reports the login
expired.
